#SETS
-collection of non-sequential elements or items (cannot use index)
-comma separated elements enclosed in {}
-Sets do not allow duplicate element/i can have duplicate element but in result it show only one element
-usage of sets:anything that is unique to store this we need sets
-Order is not maintain here
s={"apple",20}

#Basic operation in sets
1.Membership (in,not in)

2.converting tuple,list into sets vice versa
-using typecasting
-order is not maintain here cause set is non-sequential

4.Sets are mutable
set={1,2,3}
set[0]=3 -we cannot do this cause set do not use index

1.add()
syntax:set.add(item)

2.remove()
syntax:set.remove(item)

3.discard()
syntax:set.discard(item)
-it work when element is not even there in set to remove element
 while remove() work only when there is element

#Operation on sets
1.intersection
-to find common between set1 and set2
-syntax:set1 & set2 & set3
-syntax:set1.intersection(set2,set3)

2.union
-to find all the elements of set1 and set2
-syntax:set1|set2 |set3
-syntax:set1.union(set2,set2)

3.difference
syntax:set1-set2 -to find elements which are not in set2
syntax:set1.difference(set2)


#Frozen Sets
-immutable sets
-uses:unique element with no change in set and in order
syntax:variable=frozenset({item})